                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/cyberseconto/R9FIH0JGU0cP0BhCagaD9vG"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/cyberseconto/R9PtsDSJoIr3QT8tHNFMR8O"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/cyberseconto/R9FIH0JGU0cP0BhCagaD9vG"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/cyberseconto/RCKLVn1qRXBxeGqqZBT3a0Z"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/cyberseconto/R9FIH0JGU0cP0BhCagaD9vG"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/cyberseconto/RCbH9h3vdxoHpQlGVyTvWXE"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Adversaries may attempt to access cached domain credentials used to allow authentication to occur in the event a domain controller is unavailable.

On Windows Vista and newer, the hash format is DCC2 (Domain Cached Credentials version 2) hash, also known as MS-Cache v2 hash. The number of default cached credentials varies and can be altered per system. This hash does not allow pass-the-hash style attacks, and instead requires Password Cracking to recover the plaintext password.

With SYSTEM access, the tools/utilities such as Mimikatz, Reg, and secretsdump.py can be used to extract the cached credentials.

Note: Cached credentials for Windows Vista are derived using PBKDF2.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Cached Domain Credentials</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:label rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">TAC1003.005</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


